From: Julian Decker <git@deckerjulian.com>
Subject: mfd: rk808: make the syscore shutdown reach the PMIC again

rk808_syscore_shutdown() skipped a PMIC when it had a shutdown function
instead of when it had none. Every RK8xx in the list has one, so none was
ever switched off and power-off left the rails on.

Correcting the test brings more problems to light in code that has not
been running:

The RK805, RK808, RK816 and RK818 functions masked the RTC interrupts at
the RK817's addresses. 0xf9 is beyond the end of the RK808's register map
and 0x0f is not the RTC interrupt register there. Use each PMIC's own
interrupt mask register and the RTC interrupt register that rtc-rk808 uses
for them. On the RK816 the RTC bits are in the second mask register.

They touched those registers on restart as well, where the hook now also
runs. A restart has no business with them, so test the system state first.

After the write they spun for ever, also when the write failed or the PMIC
stayed up, so the kernel never got to the next power-off handler. Return an
error instead, at once if the write failed and otherwise after 100 ms. The
syscore hook logs it and machine_power_off() goes on to PSCI.

The shared steps move into two helpers. The 200 ms the RK805 waits before
its write stays. The RK801, RK809 and RK817 functions are not touched.

---
--- a/drivers/mfd/rk808.c
+++ b/drivers/mfd/rk808.c
@@ -990,6 +990,38 @@
 	return ret;
 }
 
+/* Mask and disable the RTC interrupts of the PMICs built around the RK808's RTC */
+static void rk808_close_rtc_int(struct rk808 *rk808, unsigned int int_msk_reg)
+{
+	regmap_update_bits(rk808->regmap, int_msk_reg,
+			   RTC_PERIOD_ALARM_INT_MSK, RTC_PERIOD_ALARM_INT_DIS);
+	regmap_update_bits(rk808->regmap, RK808_RTC_INT_REG,
+			   RTC_TIMER_ALARM_INT_MSK, RTC_TIMER_ALARM_INT_DIS);
+}
+
+/* Normally the rails are gone long before this wait is over */
+#define RK808_DEV_OFF_WAIT_MS	100
+
+/*
+ * A PMIC that stays up is reported as an error, so that the power-off can go
+ * on to the next handler (PSCI) instead of spinning here for ever.
+ */
+static int rk808_device_off(struct rk808 *rk808, unsigned int reg, unsigned int bit)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	ret = regmap_update_bits(rk808->regmap, reg, bit, bit);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(&rk808->i2c->dev, "Failed to shutdown device!\n");
+		return ret;
+	}
+
+	mdelay(RK808_DEV_OFF_WAIT_MS);
+	dev_err(&rk808->i2c->dev, "PMIC still up after shutdown!\n");
+
+	return -ETIMEDOUT;
+}
+
